                <pb facs="0493" n="391" sameAs="frus:frus1927v03/pg391"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">817.041/3: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to
                        the Minister in Nicaragua</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                            >Eberhardt</hi>)</head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1927-08-09T16:00:00-04:00"><hi rendition="#italic">August 9,
                                1927—4 p.m.</hi></date></dateline></opener>
                <p>120. Your 184, July 31, 4 p.m. The Department perceives no occasion now to
                    question on the grounds of <hi rendition="#italic">de facto</hi> character of
                    courts legality of acts of Supreme Court or Court of Appeals whose judges were
                    replaced by <hi rendition="#italic">de facto</hi> authorities. Your citation to
                    Cooley considered in point. See also volume one, Moore’s <hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Digest</hi>, page 54, <hi rendition="#italic">et seq.</hi></p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Kellogg</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
